+### Inverse Rendering Optimization Setup
+*   **Optimizer:** Adam [Kingma and Ba 2014] [S3].
+*   **Loss Function:** L2 loss [S3].
+*   **Number of Views:** 8 [S3].
+*   **Batch Size:** 2 [S3].
+*   **Runtime:** 30 to 90 minutes per optimization, typically 64 to 256 epochs [S3].
+*   **Hardware:** NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The Adam optimizer has $O(1)$ per parameter per step [G]. The exponential decay schedule is $O(1)$ to compute [G]. Total time per step is linear in the number of parameters [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Adam requires storing two momentum buffers per parameter [G].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Convergence takes 9 to 13 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU for typical scenes [S1]. Inverse rendering optimizations take 30 to 90 minutes on an NVIDIA RTX 4090 [S4].
-*   **Strengths:** Stable convergence for ill-conditioned problems [G]. Selective L2 decay prevents over-regularization of encodings [S2, unofficial]. Delayed decay allows initial exploration [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Standard training for neural radiance fields and implicit volume representations [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When memory is extremely constrained (use SGD) [G]. When position encodings need regularization (disable mask) [S2, unofficial].
